mechanism:
printHexu32imm reads the operand as a signed int64 and prints it with the *signed* formatHex overload, then unconditionally appends 'U':

  void NVPTXInstPrinter::printHexu32imm(const MCInst *MI, int OpNum,
                                        const MCSubtargetInfo &, raw_ostream &O) {
    int64_t Imm = MI->getOperand(OpNum).getImm();
    O << formatHex(Imm) << "U";
  }

For a prmt selector with bit 31 set, the i32 DAG constant (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFF) is sign-extended into the MachineInstr/MCOperand immediate as int64 -1. MCInstPrinter::formatHex(int64_t) emits the value in '-0x...' form for negative inputs, so the printer produces '-0x1U' (and '0x80000000' -> '-0x80000000U', '0xFFFF0000' -> '-0x10000U'). The 'U' (unsigned) literal suffix combined with a leading '-' is malformed PTX that ptxas rejects, and the printed text no longer represents the IR constant's 32-bit pattern (compare a valid selector 0x7654 -> '0x7654U'). The sibling template printHexUImm<Bits> (lines 569-579) does this correctly: it masks 'Imm &= maskTrailingOnes<uint64_t>(Bits)' and uses the unsigned formatHex(uint64_t) overload. The fix is to mask Imm to 32 bits (Imm & 0xFFFFFFFF) and/or use the uint64_t overload, matching printHexUImm. This is a misprinted operand = miscompile of the emitted text PTX. Hexu32imm is used only for prmt.b32 selector immediates (NVPTXInstrInfo.td PRMT_B32* defs), so the bug surfaces whenever a prmt selector immediate has bit 31 set.

