mechanism:
The PatFrags lump fminimumnum together with fminnum:
  def fminnum_or_fminimumnum : PatFrags<(ops node:$a,node:$b),[(fminnum ...),(fminimumnum ...)]>;
  defm MIN : FMINIMUMMAXIMUM<"min", /*NaN*/false, fminnum_or_fminimumnum>;
so ISD::FMINIMUMNUM/FMAXIMUMNUM (set Legal in ISelLowering.cpp:1045-1047) select directly to PTX min.f32/min.f64/min.f16x2/etc. with NO signed-zero fixup. PTX min/max treat -0.0 == +0.0 ("negative zero equals positive zero" in the PTX ISA min/max sections), so they do NOT order signed zeros. But the LLVM LangRef requires llvm.minimumnum/maximumnum to follow IEEE-754-2019 minimumNumber/maximumNumber where -0.0 < +0.0 deterministically: minimumnum(-0.0,+0.0) MUST be -0.0, maximumnum(-0.0,+0.0) MUST be +0.0. PROOF this is a real PTX-level miscompile, not just spec-lawyering: the SAME backend's expansion of the signed-zero-aware llvm.minimum.f64 (sm_90) emits a fixup that detects -0.0 by integer bit pattern and overrides the PTX min result:
  min.f64 %rd3, %rd1, %rd2;
  setp.eq.b64 %p2, %rd1, -9223372036854775808;   ; op1 == 0x8000000000000000 (-0.0)
  selp.f64 %rd5, %rd1, %rd4, %p2;
  setp.eq.b64 %p3, %rd2, -9223372036854775808;   ; op2 == -0.0
  selp.f64 %rd6, %rd2, %rd5, %p3;
  setp.eq.f64 %p4, %rd4, 0d0000000000000000;     ; raw min produced +0.0 ...
  selp.f64 %rd7, %rd6, %rd4, %p4;                ; ... so prefer the -0.0 operand
This fixup exists precisely because PTX min.f64(-0.0,+0.0) returns +0.0. minimumnum/maximumnum get the bare min/max with the fixup omitted, so minimumnum(-0.0,+0.0) returns +0.0 (WRONG; LangRef/opt constant-fold give -0.0) and maximumnum(-0.0,+0.0) returns -0.0 (WRONG; correct is +0.0). Affects f32, f64, f16, bf16, v2f16, v2bf16. Note: plain llvm.minnum/maxnum are EXEMPT (LangRef permits either signed zero), so mapping those to PTX min/max is fine — only minimumnum/maximumnum are wrong. Introduced by commit acff04955def "[NVPTX] Add max/minimumnum to ISel" (Aug 2025), whose message asserts the intrinsics "map directly to PTX max/min instructions" — true for the numeric/NaN behavior but not for signed zeros.

