mechanism:
The patterns
  def : Pat<(f32 (fpextend f16:$a)),  (CVT_f32_f16 $a, CvtNONE_FTZ)>,  Requires<[doF32FTZ]>;
  def : Pat<(f32 (fpextend bf16:$a)), (CVT_f32_bf16 $a, CvtNONE_FTZ)>, Requires<[doF32FTZ, hasPTX<78>, hasSM<90>]>;
select a cvt with the FTZ bit set whenever the f32 denormal mode is preserve-sign (doF32FTZ). The printed instruction is `cvt.ftz.f32.f16` (CVT_FROM_ALL appends ${mode:ftz}, line 569). Per the PTX ISA, `.ftz` flushes subnormal operands of .f32 type to sign-preserving zero; on this widening cvt it flushes the *narrow source* before widening. But every f16 subnormal (and likewise bf16) is EXACTLY representable as a NORMAL f32 value (smallest f16 subnormal = 2^-24 >> smallest normal f32 = 2^-126), so an `fpext half to float` is a value-preserving conversion whose result is never a subnormal f32. The FTZ result-flushing rationale therefore does not apply: the only effect of `.ftz` here is to corrupt the input. Moreover the f32 denormal-mode setting (`denormal-fp-math-f32`) governs f32 denormals, not f16/bf16 denormals, so flushing the f16 input based on it is wrong even under the FTZ contract. Note the f16->f64 / bf16->f64 extends (lines 2418/2421) correctly have NO ftz variant.

